---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="How it works — Devil's Advocate"
  description="How Devil's Advocate analyses argument structure, what the Toulmin frame is, and what the tool does not do."
  canonicalPath="/how-it-works"

>
  <main class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-6 pt-20 pb-16">

    <nav class="mb-12">
      <a href="/" class="text-xs text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-600 transition-colors">← The Whetstone</a>
    </nav>


    <h1 class="font-serif text-3xl sm:text-4xl text-gray-900 leading-tight mb-3">
      How it works
    </h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 mb-12">A plain-English guide to the tool and the frame it uses.</p>

    <!-- Section 1: What the tool does -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-indigo-500 uppercase mb-4">What it does</h2>
      <p class="text-base text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        When you highlight text on any article and open Devil's Advocate, the tool reads the passage
        as an argument — not as prose, not as opinion, not as fact. It identifies what position is
        being argued for, what evidence is offered in support, what assumption connects them, and
        whether any familiar reasoning patterns appear. It then writes the strongest single-paragraph
        case for the opposing position. The result is a structured view of the argument, not a
        verdict on who is right.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 2: The Toulmin frame -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-indigo-500 uppercase mb-4">The Toulmin frame</h2>
      <p class="text-base text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The tool uses the Toulmin model of argumentation — a framework from philosophy of language
        that breaks any argument into six components. You do not need to know the framework to use
        the tool, but understanding it makes the output more useful.
      </p>
      <dl class="space-y-5">
        <div class="pl-4 border-l-2 border-indigo-200">
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Claim</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            The main position being argued for. <em>"Remote work increases productivity."</em>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-4 border-l-2 border-indigo-200">
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Grounds</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            The evidence or reasons offered in support.
            <em>"Studies show output rose 13% after companies moved to remote."</em>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-4 border-l-4 border-indigo-400">
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-indigo-800 mb-1">Warrant <span class="font-normal text-indigo-500">(the load-bearing field)</span></dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-indigo-700 leading-relaxed">
            The usually-unstated assumption that connects the grounds to the claim — the thing the
            argument requires you to accept without arguing for it explicitly.
            <em>"Output measured in those studies is representative of productivity in general."</em>
            The warrant is where most arguments are actually contested.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-4 border-l-2 border-gray-200">
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Backing</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            Support for the warrant itself, when present.
            <em>"The studies used a consistent output metric validated across industries."</em>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-4 border-l-2 border-gray-200">
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Qualifier</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            A word or phrase limiting the claim's scope.
            <em>"Remote work <strong>generally</strong> increases productivity."</em>
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="pl-4 border-l-2 border-gray-200">
          <dt class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-1">Rebuttal</dt>
          <dd class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            Conditions the argument itself acknowledges as exceptions.
            <em>"Unless the role requires intensive real-time collaboration."</em>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 3: What it isn't -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-indigo-500 uppercase mb-4">What it isn't</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-3 text-base text-gray-700">
        <li class="pl-4 border-l-2 border-amber-300">
          <strong class="font-medium">It doesn't adjudicate claims.</strong> The tool analyses argument structure — not whether specific assertions are accurate. Whether the study cited is real, or whether the statistic holds up, is your job. Tier 3 retrieval (coming later) will help you check sources. The tool never asserts that a claim is true or false.
        </li>
        <li class="pl-4 border-l-2 border-amber-300">
          <strong class="font-medium">Not a political moderator.</strong> It does not decide which side of a contested question is correct. The same analysis runs on a climate-change argument and a sceptic's counter-argument. The frame is neutral; the conclusions are yours.
        </li>
        <li class="pl-4 border-l-2 border-amber-300">
          <strong class="font-medium">Not a writing improver.</strong> The tool helps you understand the argument in front of you. It does not suggest how to make your own writing more persuasive, more shareable, or better-optimised for any platform.
        </li>
        <li class="pl-4 border-l-2 border-amber-300">
          <strong class="font-medium">Not always right.</strong> The tool surfaces reasoning patterns when confidence is high (&ge;75%). It will sometimes miss things; it will sometimes flag patterns that, on closer reading, are not present. Treat it as a prompt, not a verdict.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4: What's coming -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-widest text-indigo-500 uppercase mb-4">What's coming</h2>
      <p class="text-base text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        The current extension is the Reader surface — analysis of text you encounter. A Creator
        Studio is in development: the same analysis engine, applied to your own writing before
        you post it. The studio is aimed at people who care whether their arguments are well-formed
        — students, journalists, Substackers, anyone whose work depends on reasoning clearly in
        public. No timeline commitment here; the extension ships first, and the studio follows when
        the analysis engine is battle-tested.
      </p>
    </section>

    <footer class="pt-6 border-t border-gray-100">
      <a href="/privacy" class="text-xs text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-600 transition-colors">Privacy policy →</a>
    </footer>

  </main>
</Base>
